6o9|a|Capable of being borne or endured; tolerable.	e:1xx3,28,4;1awf,27,10;1d28,18,2~1awf,27,10;1xx3,28,4;1d28,18,2
6oc|n|The hair that grows on the chin, lips, and adjacent parts of the human face, chiefly of male adults. ; The long hairs about the face in animals, as in the goat. ; The cluster of small feathers at the base of the beak in some birds ; The appendages to the jaw in some Cetacea, and to the mouth or jaws of some fishes. ; The byssus of certain shellfish, as the muscle. ; The gills of some bivalves, as the oyster. ; In insects, the hairs of the labial palpi of moths and butterflies. ; Long or stiff hairs on a plant; the awn; as, the beard of grain. ; A barb or sharp point of an arrow or other instrument, projecting backward to prevent the head from being easily drawn out. ; That part of the under side of a horse's lower jaw which is above the chin, and bears the curb of a bridle. ; That part of a type which is between the shoulder of the shank and the face. ; An imposition; a trick.	a:9si,144,3;1ujs,91,3;vs8,66,5;17n4,61,1;28oj,53,3;7yj,40,3;16r0,37,5;tml,25,2~16r0,37,5;vs8,66,5;28oj,53,3;9si,144,3;7yj,40,3;1ujs,91,3;tml,25,2;17n4,61,1	n:1ujs,85,2;28oj,60,5~28oj,60,5;1ujs,85,2	N:23r4,120,1~23r4,120,1
